          <row bottom="minor"><cell>PIG</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
          <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>RATÓN</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
          <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>CORAZÓN</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
          <row bottom="minor"><cell>DOOR</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
          <row bottom="minor"><cell>AVOCADO</cell><cell>7</cell><cell></cell><cell>8</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
          <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>ZANAHORIA</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
          <row bottom="minor"><cell>STRAWBERRY</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
          <row bottom="minor"><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell></row>
          <row bottom="medium"><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell><cell><nbsp/></cell></row>
        </tabular>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <tabular>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell>English</cell><cell>length</cell><cell>Spanish</cell><cell>length</cell><cell>ENG→SPA</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell>MILK</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>LECHE</cell><cell>5</cell><cell><m>5/4 = 1.25</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>WHALE</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>BALLENA</cell><cell>7</cell><cell><m>7/5 = 1.4</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>HOUSE</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>CASA</cell><cell>4</cell><cell><m>4/5 = 0.8</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>PIG</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>CERDO</cell><cell>5</cell><cell><m>5/3 \approx 1.66</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>MOUSE</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>RATÓN</cell><cell>5</cell><cell><m>5/5 = 1</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>HEART</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>CORAZÓN</cell><cell>7</cell><cell><m>7/5 = 1.4</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>DOOR</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>PUERTA</cell><cell>6</cell><cell><m>6/4 = 1.5</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>AVOCADO</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>AGUACATE</cell><cell>8</cell><cell><m>8/7 \approx 1.14</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>CARROT</cell><cell>6</cell><cell>ZANAHORIA</cell><cell>9</cell><cell><m>9/6 = 1.5</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>STRAWBERRY</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>FRESA</cell><cell>5</cell><cell><m>5/10 = 0.5</m></cell></row>
    </tabular>
  </solution>
</exercise>


<exercise xml:id="ex-translate-patterns" workspace="4cm">
  <statement>
    <p>Which patterns about lengths of words did you see? Any conclusions? Any rule/hypothesis? You can create a diagram or a table!</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>Students may notice several things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>The scaling factors are all different.</p></li>
      <li><p>Sometimes, the scaling factors are equal to 1.</p></li>
      <li><p>The scaling factors from ENG to SPA are often larger than 1.</p></li>
      <li>
        <p>The reverse scaling factor (SPA to ENG) is 1 over the other scaling factor.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Ask them why they think this is true! (Help them with a diagram.)</p></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Students could also look at frequencies (by range) of scaling factors, to see if there seems to be a higher concentration in a particular range, say, the range <m>[1, 2]</m>, or even a finer one like <m>[1, 1.4]</m>.</p>
    <p>Students may even find the average scaling factor (by eyeballing, or estimating it), or find the median.</p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>

<exercise xml:id="ex-translate-predict">
  <!-- <title>Predict</title> -->
  <statement>
    <p>Use the previous data and your rules to <alert>predict</alert>:
      <ol>
        <li workspace="3.5cm">
          <p>A list of words in English has <alert>100</alert> letters in total. About how many letters does it have after it is translated to Spanish?</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="3.5cm">
          <p>A list of words in Spanish has <alert>1,000</alert> letters in total. About how many letters does it have after it is translated to English?</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="3.5cm">
          <p>What's a representative <term>scaling factor</term> (or <term>multiplier</term>) to convert from # of letters in English to # of letters of a translation to Spanish? (ENG <m>\to</m> SPA)
            <md>\text{word} \xrightarrow{\;\times\, ?\;} \text{palabra}</md>
          </p>
          <!-- <image source="espanglishemos-machineEngToSpa.jpg" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>A machine that turns an English word into a Spanish word (palabra), scaling its length by an unknown factor.</shortdescription>
          </image> -->
        </li>
        <li workspace="3.5cm">
          <p>And the other way around? Scaling factor to convert from # of letters in Spanish to # of letters of a translation to English? (ENG <m>\leftarrow</m> SPA)
            <md>\text{palabra} \xrightarrow{\;\times\, ?\;} \text{word}</md>
          </p>
          <!-- <image source="espanglishemos-machineSpaToEng.png" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>A machine that turns a Spanish word (palabra) into an English word, scaling its length by an unknown factor.</shortdescription>
          </image> -->
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <assemblage component="TODO">
      <title>These solutions are more teacher notes than actual solutions.</title>
      <p>EAJ, 11Jun2026</p>
      <p>Should they go somewhere else? The <q>Analyzing the Data</q> subsection (<c>subsec-analyzing-the-data</c>) already has notes about this.</p>
    </assemblage>
    <ol marker="a">
      <li>
        <p>Anticipate different ideas, and advance their thinking:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Students may just say that the answer is a number larger than 100, but say that it's <em>impossible</em> to know!</p></li>
          <li><p>They could just guess using intuition (E.g.: 200, 150, 110).</p></li>
          <li><p>They could use a scaling factor that they liked from the data.</p></li>
          <li><p>Or: they can start thinking about an <q>ideal</q> (average, representative) scaling factor using the data collected.</p></li>
          <li><p>They can even generate more data! (New translation samples).</p></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The answer in this problem should be consistent with the answer in part (a). Start a rich discussion about scaling factors and their inverses (reverse scaling), help solidify the concepts. Introduce the idea of <q>translating back</q> and draw a diagram with 2 arrows.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Students must think about modes, averages, or medians of the data. Expect, probably, answers close to 1.25. Ask students to draw a diagram. Students' answer <em>can</em> be a small interval! For example, [1.2, 1.4]. This shows good understanding of thinking with data.</p>
        <p>Have students test their scaling factor with new translations! They can calibrate and adjust their answer based on new evidence.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Again, students need to be consistent. Ex: If the answer in part (c) is <m>6/5</m>, then the answer in part (d) should be <m>5/6</m>.</p>
        <p>If students don't connect to part (c) and start thinking about this in isolation, ask them, <q>How can we use part (c) to help us?</q></p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>
</page>


</worksheet>
